Q3. MongoDB Insert and Display

Code

   use college

   db.students.insertOne({roll_no:1,name:"Asha",dept:"IT",marks:78,address:
   {city:"Pune",state:"Maharashtra"}})

   db.students.insertMany([
      {roll_no:2,name:"Ravi",dept:"CS",marks:55,address:{city:"Mumbai",state:"Maharashtra"}},
      {roll_no:3,name:"Neha",dept:"IT",marks:91,address:{city:"Nashik",state:"Maharashtra"}},
      {roll_no:4,name:"Om",dept:"CS",marks:67,address:{city:"Pune",state:"Maharashtra"}},
      {roll_no:5,name:"Priya",dept:"ENTC",marks:84,address:{city:"Nagpur",state:"Maharashtra"},hobbies:
   ["music","chess"]},
      {roll_no:6,name:"Amit",dept:"IT",address:"Bhopal",skills:["Java","Python"]}
   ])

   show collections
   db.students.find()
   db.students.find().pretty()

Structured and unstructured documents
    Structured documents follow the same fields every time (roll_no, name, dept, marks and a
    nested address).
    Unstructured documents may miss fields, hold a different type or carry extra fields (roll_no 6 has
    no marks and a plain string address, and some students have hobbies or skills arrays). MongoDB
    accepts them because a collection has no fixed schema.
    insertOne() stores one document and insertMany() stores an array of documents. The database
    and collection are created on the first insert.
    find() with no condition displays all documents. pretty() prints them in a readable layout.

Q4. MapReduce and Cassandra

MapReduce process
MapReduce processes large data in parallel in three phases. Example: counting the students in each
department of the students collection.

  Phase                            Working in the example

  Map                              Each student record is read and the map function emits (key, 1) where key =
                                   dept. Output: (IT,1), (CS,1), (IT,1), (CS,1), (ENTC,1), (IT,1)

  Shuffle and sort                 The pairs are grouped by key. IT: [1,1,1], CS: [1,1], ENTC: [1]

  Reduce                           The reduce function adds the values of each key. Final output: IT = 3, CS = 2,
                                   ENTC = 1

Code

   db.students.mapReduce(
      function () { emit(this.dept, 1); },
      function (key, values) { return Array.sum(values); },
      { out: "dept_count" }
   )
   db.dept_count.find()

Features of Apache Cassandra
    Distributed and decentralised. Data is spread over many nodes. There is no single master node,
    so there is no single point of failure.
    High availability. Data is replicated on several nodes, and the system keeps working even if a
    node fails.
    Linear scalability. More nodes can be added without downtime, and throughput grows with the
    nodes.
    Wide-column NoSQL model. Data is stored in tables with rows and column families, with a
    flexible schema.
    Fast writes and CQL. It handles heavy writes well and offers CQL, a query language similar to
    SQL.

Application
Cassandra is used for applications with huge, fast-growing data, such as storing messages and activity
logs of a social media or messaging app.

Q5. Star Schema and Snowflake Schema

Star schema
A star schema has one central fact table (Fact_Sales) joined directly to denormalized
dimension tables. Each dimension is a single flat table, so the diagram looks like a star. It
keeps data in a denormalized form (not normalized beyond 2NF), so city, state, category and
month sit inside one table.

